<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:64:default:gcc">
<!--
    structsynth: a pair of same-typed integers read through an integer
    pointer is two elements, not a record.

    Source shapes (hand-assembled, no prologue, so each parameter arrives in
    RDI with nothing declaring its type):

        void limbs(unsigned long *p) {       // coreutils factor's powm2 shape:
            p[0] = p[0] + p[1];              // a double-limb uintmax_t *
        }

        48 8b 07        mov    rax,[rdi]
        48 03 47 08     add    rax,[rdi+8]
        48 89 07        mov    [rdi],rax
        c3              ret

        struct rec { long *next; long n; };
        void record(struct rec *r) {         // offset 0 is a pointer: a record
            r->n = *r->next + r->n;
        }

        48 8b 07        mov    rax,[rdi]
        48 8b 00        mov    rax,[rax]
        48 03 47 08     add    rax,[rdi+8]
        48 89 47 08     mov    [rdi+8],rax
        c3              ret

    Type inference already gives `limbs` an `int8 *` parameter.  A structure
    of two `int8` fields at 0 and 8 is the same bytes under a record name the
    evidence cannot tell from an array, and on a stripped binary it turns the
    correct element pointer into a name nothing can match.  Every access is
    one width on the grid 0, 8, every field would be the same integer type,
    and the parameter already points at an integer of that width, so the pass
    keeps the element pointer.  `record` reads a pointer at offset 0, which is
    heterogeneous evidence, and is still synthesized.

    Pass 1 (`option structsynth off`) and pass 2 (`option structsynth param`)
    both print `limbs` over its element pointer (asserts 1 and 2).  Pass 3
    (`param`) synthesizes `record`, the only structure minted, so it is
    `struct_0` (asserts 3 to 5).  Before the element-pair rule, pass 2 printed
    `void limbs(struct_0 *a0)` over `struct struct_0 { int8 field_0x0; int8
    field_0x8; }` and `record` became `struct_1`: asserts 1 to 4 fail there.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
488b0748034708488907c3
</bytechunk>
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100010" readonly="true">
488b07488b004803470848894708c3
